{"author":{"id":"1949ffc23f11a8cb4f8c42a45c9ad241b49570df9a18687489e9ea78d87c54a3"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1779561286,"nonce":"kKMAujOFmlOdomChOcdWrr3bTgU=","title":"Deprecate hard-coded staged models in favor of serializable graph templates","message":"## Problem\n\n`SimpleFeedback` and `SimpleStagedNetwork` still act like special backend model types even though Studio and the core graph runtime now treat the model as a graph of leaf components. This creates inconsistent semantics:\n\n- Studio authoring uses `GraphSpec` / subgraphs and normalizes runtime `SimpleStagedNetwork` payloads to `Network` wrappers.\n- `spec_to_graph()` still lowers `Network` back into `SimpleStagedNetwork` instead of executing the serialized subgraph.\n- The real training worker still extracts a supported topology and constructs a hard-coded controller/plant bridge rather than executing the authored graph.\n- `SimpleFeedbackState` is a post-hoc state view used for losses/analysis, but it can look like the canonical runtime state shape.\n- Probe/objective/data retention semantics are currently mixed with assumptions about what is already available in `state_history`.\n\nThis umbrella tracks replacing hard-coded composite model classes with serializable graph/subgraph templates and generic graph execution.\n\n## Target Architecture\n\n- The canonical portable model representation is `GraphSpec`: a human-readable, JSON-compatible graph/subgraph spec shared by core Feedbax, Studio, manifests, and project integrations.\n- Feedbax library users may construct graphs programmatically with Python builders, but builders compile to `GraphSpec` or directly to the same `Graph` structure; they must not create a second semantic representation.\n- Built-in composites such as simple feedback loops, recurrent networks, point-mass reaching models, and future biomechanical templates are graph templates/spec factories, not backend-special model classes.\n- Runtime execution uses leaf `Component`s plus `Graph`/subgraph composition. No execution path should silently lower an authored graph to `SimpleFeedback` or `SimpleStagedNetwork`.\n- Runtime state remains `equinox.nn.State` keyed by `StateIndex`. Named state views are explicit selector/view layers, not attributes that imply canonical fields such as `state.mechanics`.\n- Port values and recurrent carries are transient unless they are graph outputs, state-backed values, or explicitly retained by a probe/objective/loss requirement.\n\n## Loss, Probe, and Data-Retention Contract\n\nProbes should be retained observables, not ordinary model nodes by default. A probe may attach to a port, edge, graph output, recurrent carry, or state path. In Studio, users should be able to select a port/edge/output/state item and add a probe from an affordance similar to objective authoring.\n\nObjectives/losses must declare retention needs:\n\n- streamable per-step terms accumulate inside the scan and do not require a full trajectory;\n- finite-window streamable terms retain only a rolling window;\n- trajectory-required terms force retention of the selected observable for the trial;\n- if an objective targets a transient port and the compiler cannot stream or retain it, validation must fail with a pathful error.\n\nA separate Studio Data projection should list the state-backed trajectories that are already available plus explicit retained probes/observables. Objectives remain the view for loss terms, not general analysis data inventory.\n\n## High-Level Implementation Plan\n\n1. Define the canonical graph/template contract.\n   - Treat `GraphSpec` as the durable wire/storage format.\n   - Add template/spec-factory metadata for built-in composites.\n   - Document Python builder -\u003e `GraphSpec` -\u003e `Graph` parity.\n\n2. Replace `Network` execution lowering.\n   - Stop lowering `Network` subgraphs to `SimpleStagedNetwork` in `spec_to_graph()` and worker execution.\n   - Execute the serialized internal subgraph using leaf `GRU`, `LSTM`, `Linear`, `Mux`, recurrent wires, and explicit initializers.\n   - Preserve import/migration from old `SimpleStagedNetwork` payloads into the new `Network` subgraph spec.\n\n3. Replace `SimpleFeedback` with a graph template.\n   - Express the current feedback loop as a template-expanded `GraphSpec` of mechanics, feedback channel(s), controller, efferent/action path, and output bindings.\n   - Replace the custom `SimpleFeedbackState` view with explicit graph state selectors/views.\n   - Keep old constructors only as deprecated compatibility import/export helpers until dependent projects are migrated.\n\n4. Generalize execution and worker lowering.\n   - Make the worker consume the same graph/spec execution path as core Feedbax where possible.\n   - Any remaining optimized/specialized execution path must be explicit, validated against the graph spec, and semantically equivalent to the graph.\n\n5. Add retained-observable/probe specs.\n   - Introduce a probe/retention model that can target ports, edges, recurrent carries, graph outputs, and state paths.\n   - Integrate it with loss lowering, streaming loss, rollout artifacts, eval artifacts, and analysis availability.\n\n6. Migrate downstream project surfaces.\n   - RLRMP old constructors and recent `.eqx` artifacts need a compatibility load plan plus new graph-spec builders/manifests.\n   - Do not promise blind PyTree compatibility for arbitrary old checkpoints; reconstruct old templates only for known legacy artifacts, then emit companion graph specs/manifests where possible.\n\n## Related Issues and Expected Disposition\n\nLikely superseded or closed by this umbrella once implemented:\n\n- `9b97528` — `SimpleFeedbackState` post-hoc view redesign should resolve into explicit graph state selector/view semantics.\n- `44183de` — `SimpleStagedNetwork` LSTM execution bug should disappear once `Network` executes its serialized leaf subgraph.\n- `208a5f0` — old staged-model generality issue should be replaced by graph/subgraph component composition.\n- `c77b227` / child `614d396` — `spec_to_graph Network subgraph` cleanup is part of replacing `Network -\u003e SimpleStagedNetwork` lowering.\n\nNeeds rescope under this umbrella:\n\n- `0e1cd8c` — probes/metrics should be retained observables and selector targets, not necessarily graph nodes.\n- `2f8dd61` — parametric composites become template/spec factories over `GraphSpec`, with clear rules for derived vs manually edited internals.\n- `6116155` — typed subgraph taxonomy should distinguish causal graph templates, acausal systems, Penzai adapters, analysis DAGs, and execution/runtime capabilities.\n- `5c6b294` — RLRMP artifact loadability remains separate but depends on legacy model import/export and companion graph specs.\n\nImportant adjacent issues:\n\n- `8d94424` — Feedbax design coordination.\n- `93f79f2` — Studio design coordination.\n- `a3e46d5` — current Studio product-depth and Feedbax contract sequence.\n- `f93db6a` — recurrence contract and cycle initializers.\n- `7f02d30` — recurrent-edge Studio UX.\n- `bd5aefb` — ValueSpec/scope work should proceed after the graph/loss/probe retention contract is clear.\n\n## Non-Goals\n\n- This umbrella does not require adopting JSON as the only authoring surface; Python builders remain important.\n- This does not require probes to be ordinary graph nodes.\n- This does not require storing every port value every timestep.\n- This does not require preserving arbitrary old `.eqx` PyTree compatibility after internal graph shapes change.\n\n---\nCo-Authored-By: Codex (GPT-5) \u003ccodex@openai.com\u003e","files":null}]}